Rhat tops out at 1.00 and both effective sample sizes clear 400 comfortably across every parameter, so this fit has earned our trust the same way last chapter's did.

Now the question we actually care about: did any of this matter? Let's put the prior itself, the original flat-prior posterior from chapter 1, and this new informed posterior on the same axis and look:






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
flat_fit <- brm(
  Gfap_Intensity ~ Condition,
  data = cell_data_sub,
  seed = 123,
  file = "models/intro-applied-modeling_fit1.rds",
  file_refit = "never"
)

overlay_df <- data.frame(
  beta1 = c(
    as_draws_df(prior_check_fit)$b_ConditionMCAO,
    as_draws_df(flat_fit)$b_ConditionMCAO,
    as_draws_df(informed_fit)$b_ConditionMCAO
  ),
  curve = factor(
    rep(c("Prior alone", "Posterior (flat prior, ch. 1)", "Posterior (informed prior)"),
        each = 4000),
    levels = c("Prior alone", "Posterior (flat prior, ch. 1)", "Posterior (informed prior)")
  )
)

ggplot(overlay_df, aes(x = beta1, fill = curve, color = curve)) +
  geom_density(alpha = 0.4, linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = c("#E69F00", "#0072B2", "#D55E00")) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("#E69F00", "#0072B2", "#D55E00")) +
  coord_cartesian(xlim = c(-300, 500)) +
  labs(x = "Ischemia effect on GFAP intensity", y = "Density", fill = "", color = "") +
  Plot_theme +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom")
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Prior, flat-prior posterior, and informed-prior posterior for the ischemia effect](priors-regularization_files/figure-html/fig-priors_prior_posterior_overlay-1.png){#fig-priors_prior_posterior_overlay width=624}
:::
:::






The pale, wide curve is the prior — spread thin across a huge range, exactly as intended. The other two curves, the flat-prior and informed-prior posteriors, sit almost on top of each other, both tall and narrow, both centered right around 107. With fifteen animals showing a gap this clean, the data is already carrying the argument almost entirely on its own; our prior had an opinion, but the evidence simply outvoted it. That's not a failure of the exercise — it's exactly what should happen when you have enough good data. A prior's job is to matter *when the data can't settle things by itself*, and right now, it can.

::: callout-tip
### Frequentist equivalent

Nothing changes here on the frequentist side, and for the same reason chapter 1 flagged already: with this much data pointing this clearly in one direction, `lm()` and an informed `brms` fit are converging on the same answer as the flat-prior fit did. Where they'll actually part ways is coming up next.
:::

## Where a prior earns its keep: small samples

A prior that agrees with fifteen well-behaved animals isn't proving very much. The honest test is a dataset too small to speak clearly on its own — which is exactly the generative-model exercise from last chapter, run again with far fewer animals:






::: {#tbl-priors_sim_small .cell tbl-cap='A deliberately tiny simulated dataset (same ground truth as chapter 2)'}

```{.r .cell-code}
set.seed(2025)

true_beta0 <- 30
true_beta1 <- 107
true_sigma <- 20
n_small <- 3

sim_small <- data.frame(
  Condition = factor(rep(c("SHAM", "MCAO"), each = n_small), levels = c("SHAM", "MCAO"))
)
sim_small$Gfap_Intensity <- true_beta0 +
  true_beta1 * (sim_small$Condition == "MCAO") +
  rnorm(nrow(sim_small), mean = 0, sd = true_sigma)

gt(sim_small) |> fmt_number(columns = Gfap_Intensity, decimals = 1)
```

