Same planted truth as chapter 2 — a baseline of 30, an ischemia effect of 107 — but only three animals per group instead of eight. Let's see what a plain frequentist fit makes of this:






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(parsnip)
library(broom)

sim_small_freq <- linear_reg() %>%
  set_engine("lm") %>%
  fit(Gfap_Intensity ~ Condition, data = sim_small)

tidy(sim_small_freq, conf.int = TRUE)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
# A tibble: 2 × 7
  term          estimate std.error statistic  p.value conf.low conf.high
  <chr>            <dbl>     <dbl>     <dbl>    <dbl>    <dbl>     <dbl>
1 (Intercept)       39.5      6.72      5.88 0.00418      20.9      58.2
2 ConditionMCAO    107.       9.51     11.3  0.000351     80.9     134. 
```


:::
:::








By sheer luck of the draw, the point estimate (107.3) actually landed close to the truth this time — small samples don't always land this well, which is itself worth remembering. Now let's fit the same tiny dataset in `brms`, first with no prior stated at all (the same flat default from chapter 1), so we can isolate what *being Bayesian* buys us before we ask what an *informative prior* buys us:






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
sim_small_flat <- brm(
  Gfap_Intensity ~ Condition,
  data = sim_small,
  seed = 123,
  file = "models/priors-regularization_fit3.rds",
  file_refit = "never"
)
```
:::








Even with a flat prior — no informative opinion at all on the slope — the Bayesian credible interval (width 62) comes out wider than the frequentist confidence interval (width 53). That's not the prior doing anything yet; it's honesty about a different problem. `lm()` estimates the noise, `sigma`, once and then treats it as known when building its confidence interval. `brms` carries its own uncertainty about `sigma` all the way through the calculation. With only three animals per group, we genuinely don't know `sigma` very well — and the Bayesian interval is simply the one being upfront about that, rather than quietly assuming away a problem three data points can't solve.

Now let's actually give the prior something to do. Rather than fit several near-identical models by hand, let's dial the width of our belief about the slope — keeping the same `normal(0, ...)` shape, centered on "no assumed direction," from wide to narrow — and watch what happens to both the estimate and the interval:






::: {#tbl-priors_shrinkage_sweep .cell tbl-cap='How the ischemia-effect estimate moves as the slope prior tightens'}

```{.r .cell-code}
prior_widths <- c(150, 60, 25)
sweep_files <- paste0("models/priors-regularization_fit", 4:6, ".rds")

sweep_fits <- Map(function(sd_b, file) {
  p <- c(
    prior(normal(30, 20), class = "Intercept"),
    set_prior(paste0("normal(0,", sd_b, ")"), class = "b", coef = "ConditionMCAO"),
    prior(normal(0, 30), class = "sigma")
  )
  brm(
    Gfap_Intensity ~ Condition, data = sim_small, prior = p, seed = 123,
    file = file, file_refit = "never"
  )
}, prior_widths, sweep_files)

sweep_summary <- do.call(rbind, lapply(seq_along(sweep_fits), function(i) {
  draws <- as_draws_df(sweep_fits[[i]])
  b <- draws$b_ConditionMCAO
  ci <- quantile(b, c(0.025, 0.975))
  diag <- summarise_draws(draws, "rhat", "ess_bulk")
  diag <- subset(diag, variable %in% c("b_Intercept", "b_ConditionMCAO", "sigma"))
  data.frame(
    prior_sd = prior_widths[i],
    median = median(b),
    ci_low = ci[1],
    ci_high = ci[2],
    width = diff(ci),
    max_rhat = max(diag$rhat),
    min_ess = min(diag$ess_bulk)
  )
}))

stopifnot(max(sweep_summary$max_rhat) < 1.01, min(sweep_summary$min_ess) > 400)

gt(sweep_summary) |> fmt_number(columns = -prior_sd, decimals = 1)
```

