::: {.cell-output-display}
![Point estimate and 95% interval for the ischemia effect, from a wide frequentist fit to an aggressively tight prior](priors-regularization_files/figure-html/fig-priors_shrinkage-1.png){#fig-priors_shrinkage width=624}
:::
:::






Look at the medians as the prior tightens, from 150 down to 25: 106, 101, 40. That's a genuinely monotonic pull, away from what three noisy animals per group happened to suggest and toward the prior's center at zero — precisely the mechanism a labmate coming from a frequentist background would recognize immediately as **ridge regression**: an L2 penalty that shrinks a coefficient toward zero in proportion to how confident (small-variance) that penalty is. McElreath makes exactly this connection explicit — a regularizing prior "has the same effect as a penalized regression," damping a coefficient's tendency to overreact to whatever a small or noisy sample happens to hand it, in exchange for a small amount of bias [@mcelreath2020]. Gelman and colleagues make the identical point from the applied-modeling side: a weakly informative prior is chosen specifically to rule out implausible values without ruling out plausible ones, precisely so that a small sample can't run away with an estimate no domain expert would actually believe [@gelman2026].

Notice what *doesn't* move in lockstep: the interval width. It doesn't shrink smoothly the way the point estimate does — at a prior sd of 60 and 25, the interval is actually a bit wider than the flat-prior fit, not narrower. With only six animals total, `sigma` itself is barely identified, and pulling the slope away from what the (sparse) data wants creates a little friction that shows up as extra estimated noise rather than a tidier answer. I'd rather show you that honestly than let you walk away thinking "a more informative prior always means a narrower interval" — it doesn't. **Shrinkage of the estimate is the reliable signature of regularization here; a narrower interval is not guaranteed, and isn't really the point.**

::: callout-important
### Sensitivity check: would you actually defend `sd = 25`?

That's the honest question to ask before reporting any tightened prior, and here the honest answer is probably no — not without real, external justification (an actual prior study on GFAP fold-changes after MCAO, say, which is exactly the kind of literature-grounded number I'd want to pull from a NotebookLM lookup against Daniel's reference texts rather than invent). We tightened the prior here to *show* the shrinkage mechanism clearly, not because `sd = 25` is a prior we'd stand behind for this specific claim. That distinction — showing what a prior *can* do versus defending what a prior *should* do — is worth keeping separate every time you report one.
:::

That's the whole regularization story: with fifteen well-behaved animals, an informative prior is mostly a formality, gently agreeing with data that was already convincing on its own. With three animals per group, the exact same prior becomes the thing standing between you and a coefficient that a noisy sample handed you by accident. A frequentist fit has no equivalent lever to pull — `lm()` will report whatever the data says, confidently, regardless of how little data there was to say it. That's not a flaw in frequentist statistics; it's a design choice that assumes you'll bring your own judgment to bear on the result *afterward*. A prior just lets you bring that same judgment *into* the model itself, and get an answer that already reflects it.

We still owe this chapter one honest admission: we never checked whether either of these fitted models — the real-data one or the small-sample one — actually predicts data that looks like what we observed. We checked the prior alone, before any data arrived, and we checked that the sampler behaved. Whether the *fitted* model is any good is a different question, and it's the entire subject of the next chapter.

